WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.129
An underwater vehicle must follow a depth
command close to the surface.

00:00:05.129 --> 00:00:09.067
Waves disturb its motion, while uncertain
hydrodynamics can shift its average

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:09.658
depth.

00:00:09.658 --> 00:00:14.313
The aim is accurate mean tracking without
asking the actuators to fight every

00:00:14.313 --> 00:00:14.903
wave.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.359
With a nominal regulator, the vehicle can
remain offset from the commanded

00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:24.014
depth.

00:00:24.014 --> 00:00:28.773
At the same time, the control surfaces may
react repeatedly to wave-driven

00:00:28.773 --> 00:00:29.428
motion.

00:00:29.428 --> 00:00:33.394
The two plots distinguish a persistent
tracking error from unnecessary

00:00:33.394 --> 00:00:34.841
high-frequency control activity.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.182
The commanded depth enters an adaptive
reference layer.

00:00:40.182 --> 00:00:45.007
The LQR autopilot follows that adjusted
reference and manages the actuators.

00:00:45.007 --> 00:00:47.347
Measurements return to both layers.

00:00:47.347 --> 00:00:51.488
The adaptive layer addresses slow
uncertainty, while the regulator shapes

00:00:51.488 --> 00:00:53.000
the vehicle response.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.085
The regulator is designed using an
augmented model with frequency weighting.

00:00:59.085 --> 00:01:03.185
Control activity around the wave frequency
receives an additional penalty.

00:01:03.185 --> 00:01:06.130
This is built into the LQR design.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:11.000
It is not simply a filter added after an
already designed controller.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.871
The adaptive layer estimates the effect of
uncertainty and changes the

00:01:16.871 --> 00:01:19.065
reference supplied to the autopilot.

00:01:19.065 --> 00:01:21.258
The original command stays fixed.

00:01:21.258 --> 00:01:25.530
As the internal reference adjusts, the
mean vehicle depth approaches the

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.500
command, while residual wave-driven motion
may remain.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.839
A new depth command requires a real
maneuver.

00:01:33.839 --> 00:01:38.707
Tail planes, the sail plane, and the hover
tank contribute according to the

00:01:38.707 --> 00:01:39.699
control allocation.

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:43.444
The desired outcome is reduced rapid
actuator motion, not motionless

00:01:43.444 --> 00:01:46.309
actuators: they still respond to slower
changes.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.373
Compare the same maneuver in three
configurations.

00:01:51.373 --> 00:01:54.508
Nominal LQR can exhibit offset and
wave-frequency activity.

00:01:54.508 --> 00:01:57.280
Frequency shaping reduces the rapid
control oscillations.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.778
Adding adaptive reference management
addresses the remaining mean error.

00:02:00.778 --> 00:02:04.402
These animation traces explain the
mechanisms; they are not numerical

00:02:04.402 --> 00:02:05.000
results.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:11.320
The published work evaluates the approach
with a Joubert BB2 simulation model.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.679
Its central idea is a division of
responsibilities: frequency-shaped

00:02:14.679 --> 00:02:17.664
regulation manages wave-related actuator
activity, and adaptive augmentation

00:02:17.664 --> 00:02:19.772
addresses slow disturbances and
uncertainty.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:23.000
Quantitative results are available in the
linked papers.
